Generative AI has rapidly changed the way people interact with computers by enabling machines to create human-like text, images, audio, video, and software code. Large language models such as Mistral, Llama, and GPT are trained on massive amounts of data to learn patterns in language. When a user provides a prompt, the model processes the input and generates a response based on the patterns it learned during training. These models are now being used in education, software development, customer support, content creation, research, and many other fields.

Building a useful Generative AI application involves more than simply connecting an application to an LLM. Developers need to design effective prompts, handle user input, process model responses, and sometimes convert unstructured responses into structured formats such as JSON. Applications may also need techniques such as chunking for large documents, embeddings and retrieval for accessing external information, and evaluation methods to measure the quality of generated responses. Understanding these components helps developers build reliable applications around existing AI models rather than training a large language model from scratch.